          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. Find the area of an equilateral triangle with sidelength 6 units. [Hint: Show that an altitude bisects the opposite side. Use this to find the height.]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.824,
            0.082,
            0.926,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Try This"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.406,
            0.158,
            0.592,
            0.184
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "SUMMARY"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.207,
            0.822,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Baudhāyana-Pythagoras Theorem is one of the most fundamental theorems in geometry. It expresses the relationship among the three sides of a right-angled triangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.268,
            0.823,
            0.307
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If a, b, c, are the sidelengths of a right-angled triangle, where c is the length of hypotenuse, then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\bar { a ^ { 2 } } + \\bar { b ^ { 2 } } = \\bar { c ^ { 2 } }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.311,
            0.822,
            0.352
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In an isosceles triangle with sidelengths a, a, c, we have the relation "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a^{2}+a^{2}=2a^{2}=c^{2},\\mathrm{i.e.},c=a\\sqrt{2}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.359,
            0.824,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The number "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " lies between 1.414 and 1.415. However, it cannot be expressed as a terminating decimal. It also cannot be expressed as a fraction "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { m } { n }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " with m, n positive integers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.427,
            0.822,
            0.503
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A triple (a, b, c) of positive integers satisfying "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a^{2} + b^{2} = c^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is called a Baudhāyana-Pythagoras triple. Examples include (3, 4, 5), (6, 8, 10), and (5, 12, 13). Infinitely many such triples can be constructed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.507,
            0.822,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The equation "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a^{n} + b^{n} = c^{n}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has no solution in positive integers when "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n   \\geq   2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " This is known as ‘Fermat’s Last Theorem’. It was proven by Andrew Wiles in 1994."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
